<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<part xml:id="curriculum-guide" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Learning Map</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This section shows all the learning outcomes for Junior Cycle Mathematics.
      Throughout the book, you'll see coloured badges linking to outcomes.
      Click any badge to see what it means and where else it appears.
    </p>
    <p>
      As you work through sections, track which outcomes you've mastered.
      This helps you see your progress across the entire subject.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- UNIFYING STRAND -->
  <chapter xml:id="unifying-outcomes">
    <title>Unifying Strand</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        These outcomes apply across all strands.
        They are about how you think mathematically, solve problems, and communicate ideas.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-1">
      <title>U.1: Understand Concepts and Procedures</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Know and understand the key ideas and methods in each strand of mathematics.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Recall and demonstrate understanding of the fundamental concepts and procedures that underpin each strand.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-2">
      <title>U.2: Apply Procedures</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use the methods and procedures from each strand accurately, effectively, and in the right way.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Apply the procedures associated with each strand accurately, effectively, and appropriately.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-3">
      <title>U.3: Understand Equality</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Understand that equality means two mathematical expressions have the same value.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Recognise that equality is a relationship in which two mathematical expressions have the same value.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-4">
      <title>U.4: Multiple Representations</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Show a mathematical idea using numbers, algebra, graphs, pictures, or words. Then compare and analyse these different ways.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Represent a mathematical situation in a variety of different ways, including: numerically, algebraically, graphically, physically, in words; and interpret, analyse, and compare such representations.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-5">
      <title>U.5: Connections Within and Between Strands</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> See how ideas within one strand connect to each other, and how different strands connect to each other.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Make connections within and between strands.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-6">
      <title>U.6: Connections to the Real World</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> See how mathematics connects to real situations and the world around you.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Make connections between mathematics and the real world.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-7">
      <title>U.7: Understand the Problem</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Understand what a problem is asking. If needed, turn the situation into mathematical language.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Make sense of a given problem, and if necessary mathematise a situation.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-8">
      <title>U.8: Solve the Problem</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use what you know to solve the problem. Break it into smaller parts if needed, or simplify it sensibly.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Apply knowledge and skills to solve a problem, including decomposing it into manageable parts and/or simplifying it using appropriate assumptions.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-9">
      <title>U.9: Interpret Solutions</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Take your answer and explain what it means in the context of the original problem.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Interpret solutions to a problem in terms of the original question.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-10">
      <title>U.10: Evaluate Solutions</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Look at your answer. Does it make sense? Are there other ways to solve it? What are the limits?</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Evaluate different possible solutions to a problem, including evaluating the reasonableness of the solutions, and exploring possible improvements and/or limitations of the solutions.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-11">
      <title>U.11: Find Patterns</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Look at specific examples and find a general pattern or rule. Then test it.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Generate general mathematical statements or conjectures based on specific instances.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-12">
      <title>U.12: Build Arguments</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Build mathematical arguments and proofs using facts you already know.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Generate and evaluate mathematical arguments and proofs.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-u-13">
      <title>U.13: Explain Your Thinking</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Explain your reasoning clearly. Use the correct mathematical words and symbols.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Communicate mathematics effectively: justify reasoning, interpret results, explain conclusions, and use the language and notation of mathematics to express mathematical ideas precisely.</p>
    </section>

  </chapter>

  <!-- NUMBER STRAND OUTCOMES -->
  <chapter xml:id="number-outcomes">
    <title>Number Strand</title>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-1-a">
      <title>N.1(a): Represent Operations</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Use number lines, pictures, and grouping to show addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division with natural numbers, integers, and fractions.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Represent the operations of addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division in ℕ, ℤ, and ℚ using models including the number line, decomposition, and accumulating groups of equal size.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-1-b">
      <title>N.1(b): Perform Operations and Understand Properties</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Add, subtract, multiply, and divide. Understand why the commutative, associative, and distributive properties work.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Perform operations of addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division and understand the relationship between these operations and the properties: commutative, associative and distributive.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-1-c">
      <title>N.1(c): Index Form and Powers</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Understand and use index notation. Apply the rules of indices (powers) flexibly.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Explore numbers written in index form, including using and applying generalisations such as <m>a^p \times a^q = a^{p+q}</m>.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-1-d">
      <title>N.1(d): Factors, Multiples, Primes</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Find factors and multiples. Identify prime numbers. Find the highest common factor and lowest common multiple.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Calculate and interpret factors (including the highest common factor), multiples (including the lowest common multiple), and prime numbers.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-2">
      <title>N.2: Fractions, Decimals, Percentages</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Convert fluently between fractions, decimals, and percentages. Understand and use ratio and proportion. Solve money problems.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Investigate equivalent representations of rational numbers and use ratio and proportion in solving problems.</p>
    </section>

    <section xml:id="lo-n-3">
      <title>N.3: Patterns and Sequences</title>
      <p><strong>What this means:</strong> Find patterns in numbers. Describe them using tables and graphs. Continue sequences.</p>
      <p><strong>Official wording:</strong> Analyse numerical patterns in different ways, including making tables and graphs, and continue such patterns.</p>
    </section>

  </chapter>

</part>
